  check(r, {
    'status is 201': (r) => r.status === 201,
    'response time < 500ms': (r) => r.timings.duration < 500,
    'ride ID returned': (r) => JSON.parse(r.body).ride_id
  });

  sleep(1);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests for long-running, tenant-scoped APIs must guarantee exactly-once semantics while handling retries and high concurrency. This question probes idempotency handling, job deduplication, cross-tenant isolation, and failure resilience in a realistic pipeline.

## Key Concepts

- Idempotency keys and deduplication
- Exactly-once vs at-least-once semantics
- Background job orchestration and status visibility
- Concurrency across tenants and race-condition handling
- Failure modes and recovery (worker crash, DB outages)

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example test snippet (Node.js with axios)
const axios = require('axios');
async function testIdempotentPost() {
  const payload = { tenant: 'ace', params: { range: '30d' } };
  const id = 'idt-guest-123';
  const r1 = await axios.post('/reports', payload, { headers: { 'Idempotency-Key': id } });
